 3. Exclusive: Bots fueled outage conspiracy theories 
 
 By Sam Sabin 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Bot accounts have helped spread conspiracy theories about a wave of major tech outages over the last week, according to new research shared exclusively with Axios.

 Why it matters: Purveyors of disinformation appear to be exploiting public anxiety over the outages and fragile infrastructure, amplifying suspicions of coordinated cyberattacks and government cover-ups.

 While it's unclear who's behind these accounts, the pattern shows that when technology failures lack substantive explanations, bot networks will fill the void to manufacture panic. Catch up quick: Microsoft, Apple, JPMorgan Chase, United, Workday, the Federal Aviation Administration and the National Security Agency each faced outages over the last week. 

 Then came the conspiracy theories: When the FAA grounded flights for four hours Friday, people were quick to assume it was tied to the rollout of an AI tool at the agency. Some also tried linking the widespread Microsoft outage to recent company layoffs. Yes, but: There's no publicly established evidence that these outages were part of a coordinated cyberattack, despite the theories circulating online.

 Several companies have attributed their disruptions to technical problems, rather than malicious activity, including efforts to patch vulnerabilities to make software more resilient. Downdetector data show that while the seven days through Monday saw the highest number of weekly outages in the last 12 months, longer-term patterns are far from unprecedented, as Axios previously reported . By the numbers: Suspected bots accounted for roughly 30% of posts promoting conspiracy theories about several recent tech outages, according to data from social media monitoring firm PeakMetrics.

 During Microsoft's outage, bots accounted for 26% of conspiratorial posts. Bots accounted for 34% of conspiracy-related posts during Friday's Workday outage, compared with 27% of other posts. Likely bot accounts also made up 31% of conspiracy-related posts about Bank of America's outage and 28% of posts linking Chase's outage to other disruptions. PeakMetrics analyzed social media discussions surrounding six recent outages and classified accounts that were likely to be bots and conspiracy-related posts. Threat level: Jessica Pratt, a spokesperson for PeakMetrics, told Axios that most of the bot activity is aimed at "gaming the platforms' algorithms" and that it sees some foreign influence within that — although the company could not detail precisely how many of the bots are tied to foreign state media and governments. 

 PeakMetrics also observed a "small number of foreign state media and state-aligned accounts in the conversation," Pratt added. Go deeper: Why the internet suddenly broke all at once 

 
 
