 4. Exclusive: Corporations want say in AI rules 
 
 By Madison Mills 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Seven corporate giants are joining forces to give the businesses using AI a bigger voice in writing the rules around its deployment.

 Why it matters: Corporate America wants a better seat at the AI regulation table in Washington, which has so far mainly offered seats to frontier AI and tech companies . 

 Driving the news: America's AI Deployer Council launched today, the group shared exclusively with Axios. 

 The coalition brings together FedEx, GE Aerospace, General Motors, JPMorgan Chase, Pfizer, John Deere and United Airlines. What they're saying: Companies using AI can be left out of safety discussions even when they're responsible for managing the risks, John Bailey, an adviser to the council, told Axios. 

 One example of a challenge: AI companies have to report incidents to regulators , yet they're not required to relay that information to the businesses using the models, Bailey said. "There's a gap right now where a lot of times the deployers are just left out of that chain ... even though the deployers are the ones that are still mitigating the risk on the other side," Bailey said. Friction point: The group also hopes to make a stronger pitch on the benefits of AI to a public that has made its skepticism of AI increasingly clear, particularly through the latest election results . 

 "We need to do a better job. And I say that we as a collective, it's deployers as well as the AI developers, with surfacing the real benefits," Bailey said. Keep reading . 

 
 
